gative or zero when $G$ is a tree (or a forest).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA NOTE ON CORRELATIONS IN RANDOMLY ORIENTED GRAPHS (https://studylib.net/doc/18346497/a-note-on-correlations-in-randomly-oriented-graphs)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.5 years ago; Crawled: last month;     SVANTE LINUSSON ... This correlation persists,\n    perhaps unexpectedly, also if we first condition on {s 9 t} for any vertex t 6= s. ...     Model O (Random orientation): Every edge in G is directed either way with probability ... I was originally inspired by the so called bunkbed conjecture due to Kasteleyn, Remark 5 in [BK]. ...     where K2 is the graph on two vertices ...     Sven Erick Alm [AL1] found that a simple counterexample is the graph on four vertices\n\n# A NOTE ON CORRELATIONS IN RANDOMLY ORIENTED GRAPHS\n\n    \n    A NOTE ON CORRELATIONS IN\n    RANDOMLY ORIENTED GRAPHS\n    SVANTE LINUSSON\n    Abstract. Given a graph G, we consider the model where G is given a random\n    orientation by giving each edge a random direction. It is proven that for a, b, s ∈ V (G),\n    the events {s → a} and {s → b} are positively correlated. This correlation persists,\n    perhaps unexpectedly, also if we first condition on {s 9 t} for any vertex t 6= s. With\n    this conditioning it is also true that {s → b} and {a → t} are negatively correlated.\n    A concept of increasing events in random orientations is defined and a general\n    inequality corresponding to Harris inequality is given.\n    The results are obtained by combining a very useful lemma by Colin McDiarmid\n    which relates random orientations with edge percolation, with results by van den Berg,\n    Häggström, Kahn on correlation inequalities for edge percolation.\n    The results are true also for another model of randomly directed graphs.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA note on correlations in randomly oriented graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.2881)\nciteturn0academia18 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.5 years ago; Authors: Svante Linusson ... Given a graph $G$, we consider the model where $G$ is given a random orientation by giving each edge a random direction. ... This correlation persists, perhaps unexpectedly, also if we first condition on $\\{s\\nto t\\}$ for any vertex $t\\neq s$.\nTitle: A note on correlations in randomly oriented graphs\nAuthors: Svante Linusson\nDate: Mon May 18 15:13:41 2009\n\nGiven a graph $G$, we consider the model where $G$ is given a random orientation by giving each edge a random direction. It is proven that for $a,b,s\\in V(G)$, the events $\\{s\\to a\\}$ and $\\{s\\to b\\}$ are positively correlated. This correlation persists, perhaps unexpectedly, also if we first condition on $\\{s\\nto t\\}$ for any vertex $t\\neq s$. With this conditioning it is also true that $\\{s\\to b\\}$ and $\\{a\\to t\\}$ are negatively correlated. A concept of increasing events in random orientations is defined and a general inequality corresponding to Harris inequality is given. The results are obtained by combining a very useful lemma by Colin McDiarmid which relates random orientations with edge percolation, with results by van den Berg, H\\\"aggstr\\\"om, Kahn on correlation inequalities for edge percolation. The results are true also for another model of randomly directed graphs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSome enumerations of non-trivial composition of the differential operations and the directional derivative | Notes on Number Theory and Discrete Mathematics (https://nntdm.net/volume-23-2017/number-1/28-38/)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; Ivana Jovović and Branko Malešević ... Print ISSN 1310–5132, Online ISSN 2367–8275 ... This paper deals with some enumerations of the higher order non-trivial compositions of the differential operations and the direct